# Backend environment variables.
#
# Copy this file to backend/.env and fill in the four placeholders: the two
# Supabase values from the shared project, your own Groq key, your own Finnhub
# key. Everything not listed here has a default in code that is already correct.
#
# The sections are ordered by what it costs you to leave a variable out. Only
# section 1 tells you it is unhappy; the rest fail quietly.


# 1. REQUIRED. The process raises at import without these.
#
# supabase_client.py raises RuntimeError if either is unset, and every module
# imports it. Use the service role key, not the anon key the frontend uses:
# the backend writes past row level security.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_service_role_key_here


# 2. REQUIRED IN PRACTICE. No key means no LLM output and no error.
#
# With GROQ_API_KEY unset the client builder returns None, so a run finishes
# with no reasoning traces and no discovery candidates, and nothing in the logs
# looks wrong. Register your own free key at console.groq.com. Rate limits are
# per account, so a key shared across the team just means everyone queues.
GROQ_API_KEY=your_groq_key_here

# Optional extra accounts. The reasoning trace fan out reads GROQ_API_KEY,
# GROQ_API_KEY2 and GROQ_API_KEY3, one lane each, and asset discovery reaches
# for GROQ_API_KEY3 first and falls back to GROQ_API_KEY. Nothing reads a
# GROQ_API_KEY4 or higher. Worth setting only if you are timing full runs.
GROQ_API_KEY2=
GROQ_API_KEY3=

# Both default in code (openai/gpt-oss-20b, low). Leave them unset. They exist
# so a retired model can be swapped on a running service without a deploy:
# llama-3.3-70b-versatile was withdrawn in June 2026 and every chat model Groq
# now offers is a reasoning model, which spends part of max_tokens thinking
# before it writes. "low" keeps that to a few tokens. If a replacement model
# rejects the parameter, set it to "none" and raise the token budgets instead.
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
# GROQ_REASONING_EFFORT=low


# 3. PIPELINE FLAGS. Leave these out and you run a different pipeline.
#
# The code defaults are off and shadow, which is not what production runs.
# Anyone omitting these is on the legacy ranking path with discovery disabled,
# and their results will not reproduce anyone else's. Keep all four as below.
DISCOVERY_ENABLED=true
DISCOVERY_SHADOW_MODE=false
UNIFIED_RANKING_ENABLED=true
UNIFIED_RANKING_SHADOW=false


# 4. LOCAL FRONTEND ORIGIN, for CORS. This is also the code default, so it only
# matters if you serve the frontend on another port.
API_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173


# 5. DATA SOURCES. Each degrades quietly when blank, so a thin run is usually a
# missing key rather than a bug.

# News sentiment, plus the discovery agent's news and liquidity signals.
# Without it the news half of sentiment comes back close to empty. Free key,
# one per person, same reason as Groq.
FINNHUB_API_KEY=

# Marketaux (marketaux.com), tier 1 only supplemental news. Blank falls back to
# Finnhub. The free plan is about 100 calls per DAY at ~3 articles each, shared
# by whoever holds the key, which is why only the nightly batch calls it (deep
# pagination, cached) while user refreshes read the cache. Leave blank locally.
# Tuning: MARKETAUX_MAX_PAGES (25), MARKETAUX_CACHE_MAX_AGE_HOURS (48).
MARKETAUX_API_KEY=

# Google Cloud Natural Language, the second sentiment signal alongside VADER.
# Blank means VADER only, which is the right local default.
#
# Leave this blank unless you are working on the GCP scoring path. Since
# migration 016_nlp_budget.sql the monthly spend ledger lives in Postgres, so
# every machine pointed at the same Supabase project draws down one shared
# counter, and each process supplies its own cap from its own
# GOOGLE_NLP_MONTHLY_BUDGET. One laptop set high can spend the production month
# on its own.
GOOGLE_NLP_API_KEY=

# Production only. One call is one billed unit (text truncated to 1000 chars)
# and a run costs at most tickers x 2 sources x GCP_SENTIMENT_TOP_N, so 30
# tickers at 10 is 600 units. Billing is 5,000 units free per month, then $1.00
# per 1,000. Set below a month of runs and scoring silently drops to VADER only
# partway through the month; 40000 covers a nightly run plus manual refreshes
# for roughly $35 a month. Tuning: GCP_SENTIMENT_TOP_N (10, how many of the
# highest priority posts per ticker also get a GCP signal),
# GCP_SENTIMENT_MAX_WORKERS (10, how many of those calls are in flight at once).
# GOOGLE_NLP_MONTHLY_BUDGET=40000

# StockTwits social chatter. No key is required and blank runs unauthenticated;
# registering an app and setting a client id raises the rate ceiling.
# Tuning: STOCKTWITS_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS (1.0, process wide floor between calls).
STOCKTWITS_CLIENT_ID=

# The social sentiment trend chart. Off means the scout behaves exactly as it did
# before the feature existed: same pages, same posts, same score, no writes. Turn it
# on only after running migrations/019 in the SQL editor.
SOCIAL_HISTORY_ENABLED=false

# Three windows, and the reason they are three rather than one. The first attempt at
# this used a single lookback for both the chart's depth and the collector's, which
# put a seven day crawl on the analysis run's path: thirty tickers seeding twelve
# pages each against the one second floor was six minutes of sleeping, about ten of
# that run's eighteen minutes.
#
#   SOCIAL_DISPLAY_DAYS (7)      calendar days on the chart, and how far a BACKFILL
#                                walks. Never used to size a walk inside a run.
#   SOCIAL_ACCUMULATE_DAYS (2)   how far back a RUN walks. A run only needs enough to
#                                build today's bar, so most tickers stop on page one.
#                                Two rather than one so a ticker with nothing posted
#                                today still has a social signal.
#   STOCKTWITS_DAY_MAX_PAGES (6) the page ceiling inside a run.
#   SOCIAL_BACKFILL_MAX_PAGES (30)  the page ceiling for a backfill, far higher
#                                because nothing waits on one. A page is ~30 posts,
#                                so six pages is under two days for a busy ticker and
#                                would fill two bars of seven on exactly the assets
#                                people open.
#
# Also: SOCIAL_BACKFILL_QUOTA (30, tickers per backfill pass),
# SOCIAL_BACKFILL_MAX_SECONDS (900), SOCIAL_COLLECT_MAX_SECONDS (180, the run's wall
# clock budget), STOCKTWITS_REQUEST_TIMEOUT (6), SOCIAL_DAY_TOP_POSTS (15),
# SOCIAL_RECOMMENDATION_POSTS (5), SOCIAL_BACKFILL_RECENT_DAYS (3).
# All default correctly in code; leave them unset.


# 6. TRACING. Optional. If you switch it on, use your own project name so local
# runs do not muddy the shared trace history.
LANGSMITH_TRACING=false
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=
LANGSMITH_PROJECT=AlphaSwarm-dev-yourname


# 7. PRODUCTION ONLY. Nothing below belongs in a local .env.
#
# The nightly scheduled run (/api/analysis/run-daily). DAILY_RUN_SECRET must
# match the X-Daily-Run-Secret header on the Cloud Scheduler job; unset means
# the endpoint returns 503, which is what you want on a laptop. Generate with:
# openssl rand -hex 32. DAILY_ACTIVE_DAYS (7) only refreshes users whose last
# run is within that many days.
#
# The same secret guards /api/social/backfill, which is a SECOND Cloud Scheduler job
# at 23:00 UTC, an hour after the nightly. That order matters: discovery runs at the
# top of run-daily, so a ticker discovered tonight does not exist yet for an earlier
# job and would sit on one bar out of seven until the following night. Both jobs need
# their attempt deadline raised well above Cloud Scheduler's 180s default.
# DAILY_RUN_SECRET=
# DAILY_ACTIVE_DAYS=7


# 8. EVERYTHING ELSE: leave unset.
#
# Around sixty further knobs read from the environment, all with defaults in
# code that are the intended values. Set one only for a deliberate experiment,
# and say so in the PR, because a stray override makes your run incomparable to
# everyone else's.
#
#   RANK_*                ranking weights, floors and data sufficiency caps
#   DISCOVERY_*           candidate filters, scoring weights and decay
#   NEWS_*                tier sources, weights, shares and recency half life
#   WHALE_*               insider and institutional cache TTLs and limits
#   QUANT_*               price window and minimum universe size
#   FINNHUB_*             cache age, live top up and rate limiting
#   MAX_SCOPED_TICKERS    per run ticker ceiling (30)
#   REASONING_TRACE_TOP_N how many ranked assets get a written trace (5)
#   DESCRIPTIONS_*        entity description generation
#   STALE_RUN_MINUTES     when a held run lock is treated as abandoned (15)
#   TRACES_DIR            where run traces are written (data/runs)
#   ALPHASWARM_CACHE_DIR  local cache root (data/cache)
#
# The one worth knowing about while debugging:
# LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG
